<h1>ExpenseTracker Admin</h1>
<p><strong>Project Documentation and Technical Handover</strong></p>
<p class="small">Generated for the React administrator dashboard. Project type: Vite + React + Firebase Firestore. Date: September 2026.</p>

<h2>1. Project Overview</h2>
<p>ExpenseTracker Admin is a web dashboard for administrators to manage workforce users, groups, working schedules, GPS tracking history, and employee credits and expenses. The application reads live data from Firebase Authentication and Cloud Firestore.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Primary purpose:</strong> give administrators a secure view of personnel, group assignments, location history, monthly financial records, and login activity.</div>

<h2>2. Technology Stack</h2>
<table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Technology</th></tr>
<tr><td>Frontend</td><td>React 18</td></tr>
<tr><td>Build tool</td><td>Vite</td></tr>
<tr><td>Routing</td><td>React Router</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backend services</td><td>Firebase Authentication and Cloud Firestore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Charts</td><td>Recharts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Icons</td><td>Lucide React</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hosting configuration</td><td>Vercel configuration is included in vercel.json</td></tr></table>

<h2>3. Installation and Running</h2>
<h3>Prerequisites</h3>
<ul><li>Node.js and npm</li><li>Firebase project with Authentication and Firestore enabled</li><li>Firebase environment variables available to Vite</li></ul>
<h3>Commands</h3>
<pre>npm install
npm run dev
npm run build
npm run preview</pre>
<p>The development application normally runs on the Vite local development URL. The production build is generated in the <code>dist</code> directory.</p>
<h3>Environment variables</h3>
<pre>VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=
VITE_APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Kolkata</pre>
<p>Firebase configuration is loaded in <code>src/services/firebase.js</code>. The app warns when configuration is missing or still contains placeholder values.</p>

<h2>4. Directory Structure</h2>
<pre>src/
  App.jsx
  main.jsx
  components/
    common/       Reusable buttons, tables, modals, cards, toast, loading UI
    finance/      Monthly finance chart
    layout/       Admin shell, sidebar, topbar
    schedules/    Schedule form modal
    users/        Create user modal
  contexts/       Authentication and notification contexts
  hooks/          Authentication and notification hooks
  pages/          Dashboard, users, groups, GPS, finance, login, profile
  routes/         Application and protected routes
  services/       Firebase, auth, user, group, schedule, GPS, finance services
  styles/         Global variables, responsive rules, shared styles
  utils/          Date, format, schedule, and validation helpers
public/           Static assets
firestore.rules   Firestore security rules
vite.config.js    Vite configuration
vercel.json       Deployment configuration</pre>

<h2>5. Application Routes and Permissions</h2>
<table><tr><th>Route</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Access</th></tr>
<tr><td>/login</td><td>Firebase username/email login</td><td>Public</td></tr>
<tr><td>/dashboard</td><td>Workforce overview, group cards, status and login time</td><td>Authenticated</td></tr>
<tr><td>/users</td><td>User management and group filter</td><td>Super Admin</td></tr>
<tr><td>/users/:userId</td><td>User details and group assignment</td><td>Super Admin</td></tr>
<tr><td>/groups</td><td>Group and schedule management</td><td>Super Admin</td></tr>
<tr><td>/geo-location</td><td>Date-filtered GPS history</td><td>Authenticated with view_geo</td></tr>
<tr><td>/finance</td><td>Credits, expenses, balances and transaction history</td><td>Authenticated with view_finance</td></tr>
<tr><td>/schedules</td><td>Redirects to group schedule management</td><td>Redirect</td></tr></table>

<h2>6. Firestore Data Model</h2>
<h3>User profiles</h3>
<pre>users/{userId}
  username: string
  email: string
  user_id: string
  group_id: string
  role: string
  status: Active | Inactive
  permissions: array
  created_at: timestamp
  updated_at: timestamp
  last_login: timestamp</pre>
<h3>Groups and schedules</h3>
<pre>groups/{groupId}
  name: string
  description: string
  status: Active | Inactive
  default_schedule_id: string
  start_time: string
  end_time: string

schedules/{scheduleId}
  group_id: string
  start_time: string
  end_time: string
  active_days: array
  timezone: string
  status: string</pre>
<p>The Groups page reads every document in <code>groups</code>, including groups with zero users. It resolves the matching schedule from <code>schedules</code> and displays assigned-user counts.</p>
<h3>GPS location history</h3>
<pre>users/{userId}/geo_locations/{locationId}
  latitude: number
  longitude: number
  timestamp: Firestore Timestamp
  device_platform: string
  local_id: string</pre>
<p>The reader also accepts common mobile aliases such as <code>lat</code>, <code>lng</code>, <code>recorded_at</code>, <code>location_time</code>, <code>created_at</code>, and <code>createdAt</code>. The page filters records by selected date and schedule.</p>
<h3>Monthly credits and expenses</h3>
<pre>users/{userId}/months/{YYYY-MM}/credits/{creditId}
  credit_amount: number
  transaction_date: timestamp
  description: string

users/{userId}/months/{YYYY-MM}/expenses/{expenseId}
  expense_amount: number
  item_name: string
  transaction_date: timestamp
  local_id: number</pre>
<div class="warning"><strong>Important mobile-app rule:</strong> never write every credit to a fixed document such as <code>credits/monthly_credit</code>. Use a unique document ID or <code>add()</code>. Otherwise a later credit replaces the earlier value and the original amount cannot be recovered.</div>
<pre>final creditsRef = FirebaseFirestore.instance
    .collection('users')
    .doc(userId)
    .collection('months')
    .doc(month)
    .collection('credits');

await creditsRef.add({
  'credit_amount': creditAmount,
  'month': month,
  'transaction_date': Timestamp.now(),
  'user_id': userId,
});</pre>
<p>Correct history examples are <code>credits/credit_001</code>, <code>credits/credit_002</code>, or automatically generated IDs.</p>

<h2>7. Finance Logic</h2>
<ul>
<li>The selected month is read directly, including months that exist only through subcollections.</li>
<li>Expense totals are limited to the selected month.</li>
<li>Credits are cumulative: previous credits plus selected-month credits.</li>
<li>Balance is previous balance plus selected-month credits minus selected-month expenses.</li>
<li>The transaction table shows only records whose timestamp matches the selected date.</li>
<li>Supported amount fields include <code>amount</code>, <code>credit_amount</code>, <code>credit</code>, <code>value</code>, <code>monthly_credit</code>, and <code>expense_amount</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Example: previous credit 100,000 plus new credit 200,000 produces total credits of 300,000. A new month with no expenses keeps the carried balance and shows zero current-month expenses.</p>

<h2>8. Login Tracking</h2>
<p>After successful web login, the admin app writes <code>last_login: FieldValue.serverTimestamp()</code> to <code>users/{uid}</code>. The Dashboard and Users page display this field in the Logged In column.</p>
<p>The Flutter app must perform the same update after successful Firebase Authentication:</p>
<pre>await FirebaseFirestore.instance
    .collection('users')
    .doc(user.uid)
    .set({
      'last_login': FieldValue.serverTimestamp(),
    }, SetOptions(merge: true));</pre>

<h2>9. Security Rules Summary</h2>
<ul>
<li>Super Admin is identified by the authenticated email <code>admin@gmail.com</code>.</li>
<li>Authenticated users can read user profiles and groups according to the rules.</li>
<li>Only Super Admin can create, update, or delete users and groups.</li>
<li>GPS records can be read by Super Admin, the owner, or users with <code>view_geo</code>.</li>
<li>Finance records can be read by Super Admin, the owner, or users with <code>view_finance</code>.</li>
<li>Field workers can create their own GPS records.</li>
<li>Location records are immutable for normal users after creation.</li>
</ul>
<p>The authoritative rules are in <code>firestore.rules</code>. Deploy rule changes through the Firebase CLI or Firebase Console according to the project workflow.</p>

<h2>10. Main Services</h2>
<table><tr><th>File</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
<tr><td>src/services/firebase.js</td><td>Initializes Firebase app, Auth, Firestore, and secondary Auth.</td></tr>
<tr><td>src/services/authService.js</td><td>Login, logout, user creation, password changes, and login timestamps.</td></tr>
<tr><td>src/services/userService.js</td><td>Reads users and updates user group/status.</td></tr>
<tr><td>src/services/groupService.js</td><td>Reads, creates, edits, counts, and deletes groups and default schedules.</td></tr>
<tr><td>src/services/scheduleService.js</td><td>Reads and updates group schedules.</td></tr>
<tr><td>src/services/geoLocationService.js</td><td>Reads, normalizes, filters, sorts, and summarizes GPS records.</td></tr>
<tr><td>src/services/financeService.js</td><td>Reads credits/expenses and calculates totals, carry-forward balances, and transactions.</td></tr></table>

<h2>11. Maintenance and Troubleshooting</h2>
<h3>Group does not appear</h3><p>Confirm the document exists in <code>groups</code>, refresh the page, and verify Firestore read permission. The current pages do not intentionally hide zero-user groups.</p>
<h3>GPS table is empty</h3><p>Confirm the path is <code>users/{uid}/geo_locations/{locationId}</code>, that the selected date matches the stored timestamp in Asia/Kolkata, and that latitude/longitude fields contain valid numbers. The reader has a full-collection fallback for string timestamps.</p>
<h3>Credit total is replaced</h3><p>Inspect the mobile writer. If it uses <code>.doc('monthly_credit').set()</code>, replace it with <code>.add()</code> or unique document IDs. Firestore cannot restore values that were overwritten.</p>
<h3>Logged In shows --</h3><p>The user profile does not contain a supported login field. Write <code>last_login</code> after successful Flutter login using <code>FieldValue.serverTimestamp()</code>, then refresh the admin page.</p>

<h2>12. Validation</h2>
<p>The project production build is validated with:</p>
<pre>npm run build</pre>
<p>Build output is written to <code>dist</code>. The current project has no automated test script configured in <code>package.json</code>; behavioral verification should be performed against the Firebase project using test users and sample records.</p>

<h2>13. Recommended Next Steps</h2>
<ol>
<li>Update the Flutter credit writer to use unique credit documents.</li>
<li>Update Flutter login to write <code>last_login</code>.</li>
<li>Confirm Firebase rules are deployed and match <code>firestore.rules</code>.</li>
<li>Verify one user, one group, one GPS record, one credit, and one expense end to end.</li>
<li>Consider adding automated service tests for finance totals and date filtering.</li>
</ol>

<p class="small">End of ExpenseTracker Admin project documentation.</p>
